 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Determined by the rasterization and backward pass of the Gaussian splatting algorithm, plus the overhead of computing additional PBR or depth losses.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard 3DGS memory usage plus buffers for additional material properties (roughness, metallic) and potentially MLP weights for deformation [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High-fidelity reconstruction of dynamic scenes (ToRF) or physically accurate materials (Phys3DGS) when hyperparameters are tuned to balance stability and detail.
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to hyperparameter choices; incorrect regularization strengths can lead to instability or over-smoothing. Requires careful tuning of learning rates for different SH degrees and material properties.
+*   **Stability:** High regularization strengths ($\lambda=1.0$) are critical to prevent gradient overflow caused by erroneous Gaussians [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Convergence:** The MLP learning rate decay over 60,000 iterations ensures stable optimization for dynamic scenes [S1].
+*   **Hardware/Resolution:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Prevents training collapse in inverse rendering tasks by managing material-lighting ambiguity and visibility errors [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of per-parameter learning rates; incorrect values can lead to instability [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-*   **ToRF (Time of the Flight of the Gaussians):** Introduced in CVPR 2025, this method optimizes depth indirectly using C-ToF data, requiring specific loss weights and MLP decay schedules [S1].
-*   **Phys3DGS:** Introduced in arXiv 2024, this method extends 3DGS to inverse rendering with PBR materials, necessitating distinct learning rates for material parameters and smoothness regularizers to prevent instability [S2, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** The settings are derived from implementations of Phys3DGS [S2, unofficial] and Time of the Flight of the Gaussians (ToRF) [S1].
+*   **History:** These settings were developed to address instability in differentiable volume rendering and inverse rendering, specifically regarding the optimization of physically based attributes (roughness, metallic) and dynamic deformations [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in 3D Gaussian Splatting variants for inverse rendering and dynamic scene reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
